<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Historical Sites and Tourism</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Take a Trip to the Panama Canal</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of detailed descriptions in personal stories. Interactive: Interpret peer feedback in collaborative projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify details from audio stories about Indigenous traditions in Panama (e.g., "What materials are used to make the Emberá clothing?" "What instruments are played during Guna ceremonies?" "What foods are prepared for traditional Ngäbe-Buglé festivals?").
            Can interpret cultural descriptions and provide feedback (e.g., "I think we can add a description of the traditional dance that the Emberá people perform at their festivals. It's a big part of their celebrations.").
            Can interpret peer feedback on a project about Indigenous cultures by summarizing key suggestions and identifying areas for improvement (e.g., "My classmate liked the cultural elements in our project but suggested adding more pictures of the Guna people wearing traditional clothing to better showcase their culture.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of new vocabulary and language structures related to "Historical Sites and Tourism" and "Take a Trip to the Panama Canal".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various large images of the Panama Canal (ships transiting, Miraflores Locks, visitors). Students are asked to call out what they see and what they know about the place. The teacher plays a short audio clip with sounds of a ship's horn, water, and tourists talking, asking: "What sounds do you hear? Where do you think these sounds come from? What do these places look like?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 3-4 key vocabulary words: `Panama Canal`, `historical sites`, `visitor`, `explore`. The teacher shows a map of Panama, pointing to the Panama Canal and other historical sites, saying: "This is the `Panama Canal`. It's one of Panama's most important `historical sites`. Many `visitors` come here to `explore` its history." The teacher models pronunciation, focusing on stress in `historical` and `visitor` and minimal pairs like /ɪ/ in `sit` (e.g., in visitor) and /iː/ in `seat` (if applicable, e.g., for 'see').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the Panama Canal a very old place or a very new place?" (Expected answer: An old/historical place.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you go to a museum, are you a guide or a visitor?" (Expected answer: A visitor.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language and content related to the scenario "Historical Sites and Tourism" through authentic audio material, ensuring connection to the "Listening" skill focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording (e.g., a short personal anecdote/podcast excerpt, approx. 1-1.5 minutes) from a `visitor` describing their trip to the `Panama Canal` and other `historical sites` in Panama. The speaker uses `present simple` to describe current features, `simple past` for their experiences, and `going to` for future plans. The recording incorporates vocabulary such as `landmarks`, `architecture`, `attract`, `tour`, `learn`, `informative`, `heritage`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of detailed descriptions in personal stories.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students answer: "What is the main topic of the story? Is it about food, music, or travel?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Details - True/False):</b> The teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements based on the audio details (e.g., "The speaker visited the Panama Canal yesterday." "The speaker is `going to` see Casco Viejo next month."). Students listen again and mark T or F.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Matching):</b> The teacher asks students to match places mentioned with activities or descriptions from the audio (e.g., "Panama Canal" -> `saw massive ships transit`, "`Casco Viejo`" -> `admired beautiful architecture`, "old fort" -> `learned about Panama's heritage`).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy-focused activities designed to reinforce listening for specific grammatical structures and target vocabulary in preparation for communicative use.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple and simple past for describing places and experiences; future tense with "going to" for plans; possessive forms with 's.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> `historical sites`, `Panama Canal`, `Casco Viejo`, `landmarks`, `architecture`, `heritage`, `destination`, `visitor`, `museum`, `tour`, `explore`, `travel`, `preserve`, `describe`, `inform`, `renowned`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice pronouncing stress in multisyllabic words like `cultural`, `historical`, `information`, `destination`.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Listening Gap-fill):</b> The teacher plays specific sentences or short segments from the audio where target vocabulary or grammar structures are missing. Students listen and fill in the blanks on a worksheet. E.g., "The Panama Canal ___ (attracts) many `visitors`." "Last year, we ___ (visited) `Casco Viejo` and ___ (explored) its unique `architecture`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Sentence Completion):</b> Students listen to incomplete sentences from the audio and complete them with appropriate adjectives or verbs. E.g., "The guide's presentation was very ___ (informative)." "We are `going to` ___ (travel) to the `Cauway` next." The teacher pauses the audio for students to write.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Listen and Summarize Keywords):</b> In pairs, students listen to a new, short audio description of a specific historical site (e.g., `Panama Viejo` `ruins`). Each student writes down 3-4 keywords or phrases they hear. Then, they compare their lists, saying: "I heard 'ancient `ruins`.' What did you hear?" "I heard 'quiet `destination`.'" This fosters interpretation of peer feedback and sociolinguistic engagement in conversation.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students actively use the acquired language in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency and collaborative interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Historical Site Listening Challenge – Travel Agents"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4). The teacher plays 2-3 distinct, short audio recordings (e.g., simulated voicemails from "tourists" or short travel blog snippets) describing visits or plans related to *different* Panamanian `historical sites` (e.g., `Portobelo Forts`, `Casco Viejo`). Each recording will include details, use target vocabulary, and grammatical structures (present simple, simple past, `going to`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After each audio, groups collaboratively listen and create a "Travel Summary Report" (a short bulleted list) for that specific audio, noting:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which `historical site(s)` were mentioned?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did the `visitor` do/see there? (Use simple past, e.g., "`explored` the `fort` `ruins`").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What are the visitor's future plans (if any)? (Use `going to`, e.g., "She is `going to` `visit` the `museum` tomorrow.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Key adjectives used to `describe` the site (e.g., `preserved`, `ancient`, `attractive`, `renowned`).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The groups discuss and agree on their summaries, demonstrating their ability to `interpret peer feedback` to reach a consensus. This activity reinforces the pragmatic function of `understanding intent and context` of the speaker.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to gauge student comprehension and application of listening skills and target language. Includes student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher collects the "Travel Summary Reports" produced by each group in Stage 4. The teacher also observes groups during the "Historical Site Listening Challenge" for active participation and collaboration.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can accurately identify `historical sites` mentioned in the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can extract specific details (activities, descriptions) from the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of present simple, simple past, and 'going to' in summarization.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., `landmarks`, `architecture`, `tourist-friendly`, `heritage`) accurately in the summaries.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages collaboratively in group discussion and `interprets peer feedback` to form a coherent summary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two questions before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new detail you learned about a `historical site` in Panama from today's listening activities?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of information in the audio stories was easiest for you to understand, and what was most challenging?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson's effectiveness, identifying areas for improvement and connecting to the next lesson in the sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"On a scale of 1-5 (1=not well, 5=very well), how confident do you feel about understanding descriptions of `historical sites` in English after today's lesson? What made you feel this way?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new vocabulary word from today's lesson do you think is most useful for talking about tourism, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The initial `Warm-up` activity successfully activated prior knowledge and introduced the theme. The `Presentation` audio was appropriate for the A2.4 level, and most students grasped the gist and some specific details."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The `Preparation` stage activities, particularly the gap-fill and sentence completion, effectively reinforced target vocabulary and grammar. However, some students still showed difficulty with differentiating between minimal pairs in certain words, which requires further focused pronunciation practice."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"During `Performance`, group collaboration was generally good, and students demonstrated the ability to extract relevant information, though some groups needed scaffolding to use the `going to` structure accurately for future plans."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"For the next lesson, I will ensure clearer instructions for the collaborative task and provide a quick review of the `going to` future tense. I will also incorporate more visuals during listening activities to aid comprehension for all learners."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), we will read a text about the `Panama Canal` and other `historical sites`, applying the vocabulary and background knowledge we gained today to understand written descriptions and facts."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>